The NHS describes magnesium as a mineral that helps turn the food we eat into energy. It also helps make sure the parathyroid glands, which produce hormones important for bone health, work normally.

According to the NHS, magnesium is found in a wide variety of foods, including spinach, nuts and wholemeal bread. The NHS states that people should be able to get all the magnesium they need from a varied and balanced diet. It gives the adult daily amount as 300mg for men and 270mg for women aged 19 to 64.

The NHS states that taking more than 400mg of magnesium for a short time can cause diarrhoea. It adds that there is not enough evidence to say what the effects of taking high doses for a long time might be. NHS 111 Wales states that 400mg or less a day of magnesium from supplements is unlikely to cause any harm.

According to NICE evidence summary ESUOM4, oral magnesium glycerophosphate does not have marketing authorisation in the UK for preventing recurrent hypomagnesaemia or for any other indication, so it is an unlicensed medicine in the UK. The same summary states that, in line with General Medical Council guidance, the prescriber is responsible for judging the patient's clinical need and whether oral magnesium glycerophosphate is suitable.

A local NHS prescribing list, the Cambridgeshire and Peterborough Formulary, lists magnesium glycerophosphate chewable tablets as "licensed" and the liquid form as "unlicensed." This differs from the NICE wording, and the sources do not explain why. No BNF page was found, and none of the retrieved sources says whether magnesium is prescription-only.
