On its role, the NHS states that colecalciferol, a form of vitamin D, helps the body take in more calcium and phosphorus from food. A local NHS leaflet from NHS Gloucestershire says vitamin D works with nutrients such as calcium and phosphorus for healthy bones, muscles and teeth.

On food sources, the NHS lists oily fish (salmon, mackerel, herring and sardines), eggs, red meat and fortified foods such as fat spreads and some breakfast cereals. The NHS also states that it would be difficult to get the amount of vitamin D needed from food alone, and that most vitamin D comes from the action of the sun on the skin.

On regulatory status, the NHS states that a high-strength loading dose course of vitamin D is only available on prescription. It also states that supplements are not tested in the same way as pharmacy and prescription medicines.

