A British Dietetic Association factsheet, hosted on the Sheffield Children's NHS library site, gives average iodine amounts per portion and notes that "actual iodine content will vary." Its figures are:
- Cow's milk (200ml): 50–100 mcg
- Yoghurt (150g): 50–100 mcg
- Cheese (40g): 15 mcg
- Haddock (120g): 390 mcg
- Cod (120g): 230 mcg
- Plaice (130g): 30 mcg
- Salmon fillet (100g): 14 mcg

The factsheet also states that some milk-alternative drinks are fortified with iodine, but not all are (https://library.sheffieldchildrens.nhs.uk/iodine). Because this source is not published by the NHS, NICE or BNF, it is not included in the structured record below.

The NHS states that "Adults need 140 micrograms (μg) of iodine a day" (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iodine).

