The NHS states that vitamin D helps the body absorb calcium and is important for both strong muscles and healthy bones. According to the Robert Jones and Agnes Hunt Orthopaedic Hospital NHS Trust, it allows mineralisation of bones and maintains calcium levels in the blood, which the trust describes as essential for the normal function of the nerves, muscles (including the heart) and other organs.

The NHS lists oily fish (such as salmon, sardines and mackerel), egg yolks, and fortified foods (such as some fat spreads and breakfast cereals) as good food sources. The same NHS page notes that from late March or early April to the end of September, vitamin D can be made from sunlight on the skin. The North & East Devon Formulary describes natural food sources as very limited and mostly of animal origin, such as oily fish, red meat and egg yolk. It notes that some, but not all, brands of malted milk drinks contain added vitamin D.

On the question of daily need, the NHS states that "Adults need 10 micrograms (400 International Units or IU) of vitamin D a day." North Cumbria Integrated Care gives the same figure of 10 micrograms a day for adults, including pregnant and breastfeeding women and people at risk of vitamin D deficiency. The NHS also reports that the Department of Health and Social Care recommends a daily 10 microgram supplement throughout the year for adults and children over 4 who are not often outdoors, live in an institution such as a care home, or usually wear clothes that cover most of their skin when outdoors. The NHS states a maximum of 100 micrograms (4,000 IU) a day for adults, including pregnant and breastfeeding women and the elderly. No NHS, NICE or BNF source in the evidence states the UK legal category of vitamin D.

