The NHS lists meat, shellfish, dairy foods such as cheese, bread, and cereal products such as wheatgerm as good sources of zinc. According to the NHS, adults need about 9.5mg of zinc a day for men aged 19 to 64 years and 7mg a day for women, and people should be able to get all the zinc they need from their daily diet. Under the heading "What does the Department of Health and Social Care advise?", the NHS states that no more than 25mg of zinc supplements should be taken a day unless advised by a doctor, and the NHS 111 Wales page gives the same 25mg figure. The NHS also states that taking high doses of zinc reduces the amount of copper the body can absorb, which can lead to anaemia and weakening of the bones. The NHS page was last reviewed on 03 August 2020. The retrieved UK sources do not describe what zinc does in the body, because the relevant text was not captured. The BNF lists zinc sulfate as a drug, in effervescent tablet form, with oral solution and solution for injection available from special-order manufacturers.

